<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medang Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Medang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Medang kingdom, also known as Mataram, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-buddhist">Hindu-Buddhist</a> kingdom of central and later eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> from the eighth to the early eleventh century. It is associated with the building of monuments such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borobudur">Borobudur</a> and Prambanan. It was a rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, and conflict between the two shaped the region's politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medang-kingdom.jpg" alt="Medang Kingdom" width="320" height="134" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gunawan Kartapranata · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mataram_Kingdom_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Javanese kingdom of <b>Medang</b> under Dharmawangsa attacked Sumatra around 990 CE, Srivijaya withstood the assault, and in 1016 the Medang court was destroyed by a rebellion of the vassal ruler of Wurawari, an event many historians attribute to Srivijayan backing or regard as a Srivijayan invasion…</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
